## A Metallic Surprise in Your Morning Sweetener

It's no surprise that honey is a beloved staple in many households, but Dakota Honey Company managed to bring an unwelcome twist to their recently recalled product line. On May 20, 2026, a Class II recall was initiated for their Spreadable Spun Honey varieties after reports surfaced regarding foreign objects, specifically stainless steel dust and plastic shreds, contaminating their sweet creations.

The affected products include various flavors of spun honey--Salted Caramel, Jalapeno, Cinnamon-Infused, and Original--packaged in 4 oz and 12 oz plastic jars. To add insult to injury, these jars even come with a stirring instruction, as if the consumer should expect to emulsify the contaminants themselves.

## What Went Wrong?

The recall highlights a critical lapse in Dakota Honey Company's manufacturing process. The presence of stainless steel and plastic debris suggests issues with equipment maintenance or packaging integrity. While the recall is classified as Class II--indicating the likelihood of adverse health consequences is remote--the mere thought of such contaminants is enough to sour the appetite.

## A Broader Trend

This recall isn't an isolated incident. Foreign objects in food products account for 91 recalls this year, a notable statistic that serves as a reminder for manufacturers to tighten their quality control protocols. Dakota Honey Company's misstep is emblematic of a larger issue within the industry, where safety sometimes takes a backseat to production efficiency.

## A Word to the Wise

Consumers are advised to check their pantry for Dakota Honey products bearing the specified UPCs and lot numbers. If you happen to own any, return them to the place of purchase for a refund. More importantly, consider whether the honey's sweetness is worth the risk.

## Conclusion

Dakota Honey Company's recall serves as a cautionary tale for both consumers and manufacturers. While honey's natural purity is its hallmark, lapses in production standards can lead to serious consequences. For now, it might be wise to stick with brands that prioritize your safety over cost-cutting measures.
